import MathlibLattice.VersionSpace.Concept
import MathlibLattice.Order.Belief.Basic
import MathlibLattice.Order.Concept.Reduced
import MathlibLattice.Order.Concept.Incremental
import MathlibLattice.Height.Filtration
import MathlibLattice.Transform.Action
import Mathlib.Tactic.TFAE

-- The library's files carry no copyright header.
set_option linter.style.header false

/-!
# The generalization query

Let `r : α → β → Prop` be a formal context whose objects are statements, refuted ones included,
and whose attributes are descriptions. Given positive objects `Q` and negative objects `N`, the
query asks what `Q` can be generalized to. Its answers are the concepts consistent with `Q` and
`N`: the concepts whose extent contains every positive object and no negative object. They form
the version space of `Q` and `N` in the concept lattice, with the extent map as extension map
(Mitchell 1982; Ganter–Kuznetsov 2003), so the theorems below are the version space and concept
lattice theorems of the other files applied to this setting.

Consistent concepts exist if and only if the concept generated by `Q` contains no negative
object, and then that concept is the least consistent concept, the least general generalization
of `Q`; with no chain condition, the consistent concepts are the concepts above it whose extent
misses `N`. The consistent concepts are order-convex. If the concept lattice has no infinite
increasing chain, in particular for a finite context, every consistent concept lies below a
maximal one, and the consistent concepts are exactly the concepts lying between the least
consistent concept and a maximal one. The certain predictions are the extent of the least
consistent concept, and the possible predictions are the union of the maximal extents.

Read on intents, a concept is consistent when its intent consists of attributes common to `Q`
and every object of `N` lacks one of them. The attribute concept of `b` is consistent exactly when
`b` is common to `Q` and no object of `N` has `b`, so the answers are these attribute concepts if
and only if every answer is an attribute concept, a condition on the answers alone; the least
answer is the attribute concept of `b` exactly when `b` is common to `Q` and every object having
`b` lies in its extent. The consistent concepts are the concepts generated
by the sets of attributes separating `Q` from `N`, and the maximal consistent concepts, Mitchell's
general boundary, are the maximal concepts generated by the minimal separating sets, when every
separating set contains a minimal one (for finitely many attributes or finitely many negative
objects). For a single negative object `n` they are the maximal attribute concepts of the
attributes common to `Q` that `n` lacks. The query is equivariant under a group acting on an
invariant context.

A concept is unstated if it is not the object concept of any object. For finitely many objects
this means that its mass is zero. An unstated concept is a proper join, the least upper bound of
the concepts strictly below it; a stated concept can be a proper join too, and every proper join
is unstated exactly when every object concept is completely sup-irreducible, as in a reduced
context. The normal form of a concept is the set of completely inf-irreducible concepts above it;
it consists of attribute concepts of attributes in the intent, with no chain condition. Every
concept is the meet of its normal form, and the normal form determines the concept, exactly when
the completely inf-irreducible concepts are meet-dense, in particular when the concept lattice
has no infinite increasing chain.

At a finer level of description the least general generalization becomes more specific and every
answer stays an answer; this applies to the levels of the height filtration. Adding a context `r'`
of statements with the same descriptions forms the subposition of the two contexts. Its answers
are the concepts whose intent is `c.intent ∩ d.intent`, for an old answer `c` and an answer `d` of
`r'`, such that no negative object has every attribute of this intent; so there are at most as many
as the product of the two numbers of answers. Adding one statement with description set `S` is the
case of the one-object context, whose intents are `Set.univ` and `S`. Adding a statement
that is neither positive nor negative, with description set `S`, keeps every answer, adds only
answers whose intent is `B ∩ S` for the intent `B` of an old answer, does not change whether
answers exist, and at most doubles their number. If it is added as a positive object, the
answers are the concepts with intent `B ∩ S` for the intents `B` of old answers such that no
negative object has every attribute of `B ∩ S`, and the least one has intent
`upperPolar r Q ∩ S`; if it is added as a negative object, the answers are the old answers whose
intent is not contained in `S`. Stating an answer,
adding a statement whose description set `S` is an intent, changes no intent: the concept lattice
is unchanged up to an intent-preserving isomorphism, and the concept with intent `S` becomes the
object concept of the new statement, with positive mass.

## Main definitions

* `MathlibLattice.Query.consistentConcepts r Q N`: the concepts consistent with `Q` and `N`.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N`: the maximal consistent concepts.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.certainPredictions r Q N`, `MathlibLattice.Query.possiblePredictions r Q N`:
  the objects in the extent of every, respectively some, consistent concept.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.separatingSets r Q N`: the sets of attributes common to `Q` such that every
  object of `N` lacks one of them.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.IsUnstated r c`: `c` is not an object concept.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.normalForm r c`: the completely inf-irreducible concepts above `c`.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.addObjectConcept S c`: the concept with the intent of `c` in the context
  extended by one object with attribute set `S`.
* `MathlibLattice.Query.addObjectOrderIso`: when `S` is an intent, the isomorphism of concept
  lattices given by `addObjectConcept S`.

The least consistent concept is Mathlib's `Concept.ofObjects r Q`; coverage and mass are
`Concept.coverage` and `Concept.mass`.

## Main statements

* `nonempty_consistentConcepts_iff`, `isLeast_ofObjects`: existence of answers and the least
  answer.
* `consistentConcepts_eq_Ici_inter`, `ordConnected_consistentConcepts`,
  `isAntichain_maximalConsistentConcepts`, `exists_mem_maximalConsistentConcepts_le`,
  `consistentConcepts_eq_biUnion_Icc`: the shape of the set of answers.
* `mem_consistentConcepts_iff_intent`, `consistentConcepts_eq_image_ofAttributes`: the answers
  read on intents.
* `ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff`, `consistentConcepts_eq_image_ofAttribute_iff`,
  `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_subset_range_ofAttribute`,
  `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_iff_subset_range_ofAttribute`, `ofObjects_eq_ofAttribute_iff`:
  exactly when the answers, the most general answers and the least answer are attribute concepts.
* `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal`, `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_finite`,
  `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_finite_negatives`, `encard_maximalConsistentConcepts_le`,
  `maximalConsistentConcepts_singleton`: the most general answers from minimal separating sets.
* `certainPredictions_eq`, `possiblePredictions_eq`: the predictions of the query.
* `consistentConcepts_anti`: more evidence leaves fewer answers.
* `consistentConcepts_smul`: the query is equivariant.
* `isUnstated_iff_mass_eq_zero`, `IsUnstated.not_completelySupIrred`,
  `forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred`,
  `not_forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred`: unstated concepts, mass and proper joins.
* `exists_mem_intent_ofAttribute_eq`, `normalForm_eq_image_ofAttribute`: normal forms consist of
  attribute concepts.
* `sInf_normalForm`, `normalForm_inj`, `forall_sInf_normalForm_eq_iff`,
  `injective_normalForm_iff`, `sInf_normalForm_of_wellFoundedGT`: every concept has a unique
  normal form exactly when the completely inf-irreducible concepts are meet-dense.
* `extent_ofObjects_subset`, `extent_ofObjects_level_subset`: finer descriptions give a more
  specific least general generalization.
* `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff`, `encard_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le`: the answers
  after adding a context of statements, some of them positive and some negative.
* `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff`, `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_positive_iff`,
  `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_negative_iff`, `card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le`: the answers
  after adding statements that are neither positive nor negative, all positive, or all negative;
  `not_card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le` shows that the count needs a finite added context.
* `mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff`, `card_consistentConcepts_addObject_le`: the answers
  after adding a statement that is neither positive nor negative.
* `mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_positive_iff`, `mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_negative_iff`:
  the answers after adding a positive or a negative statement.
* `isIntent_addObject_iff_of_isIntent`, `addObjectConcept_eq_ofObject_none`,
  `isUnstated_addObjectConcept_iff`, `mass_addObjectConcept_of_intent_eq`: stating an answer.

## References

* T. M. Mitchell, *Generalization as search*, Artificial Intelligence 18, 1982.
* B. Ganter and R. Wille, *Formal Concept Analysis: Mathematical Foundations*, Springer, 1999.
* B. Ganter and S. O. Kuznetsov, *Hypotheses and version spaces*, ICCS 2003, LNCS 2746.
* M. Grabisch, *Belief functions on lattices*, Int. J. Intell. Syst. 24 (2009).
-/

namespace MathlibLattice.Query

variable {α β β' D : Type*}

/-! ### Consistent concepts and the predictions of the query -/

section Answers

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {Q Q' N N' : Set α} {c : Concept α β r}

/-- The concepts of `r` consistent with the positive objects `Q` and the negative objects `N`:
the concepts whose extent contains every positive object and no negative object. This is the
version space of `Q` and `N` in the concept lattice, with the extent map as extension map
(Mitchell 1982; Ganter–Kuznetsov 2003). -/
abbrev consistentConcepts (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) : Set (Concept α β r) :=
  VersionSpace.versionSpace Concept.extent Q N

/-- The maximal consistent concepts: the consistent concepts that lie below no other consistent
concept, the most general answers to the query. -/
abbrev maximalConsistentConcepts (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) : Set (Concept α β r) :=
  {c | Maximal (· ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N) c}

/-- The certain predictions of the query: the objects that lie in the extent of every concept
consistent with `Q` and `N`. -/
abbrev certainPredictions (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) : Set α :=
  VersionSpace.certainPredictions Concept.extent (consistentConcepts r Q N)

/-- The possible predictions of the query: the objects that lie in the extent of some concept
consistent with `Q` and `N`. -/
abbrev possiblePredictions (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) : Set α :=
  VersionSpace.possiblePredictions Concept.extent (consistentConcepts r Q N)

/-- Some concept is consistent with `Q` and `N` if and only if the extent of the concept
generated by `Q`, the set of objects having every attribute common to the objects of `Q`,
contains no object of `N`. -/
theorem nonempty_consistentConcepts_iff :
    (consistentConcepts r Q N).Nonempty ↔ Disjoint (Concept.ofObjects r Q).extent N :=
  VersionSpace.nonempty_versionSpace_extent_iff

-- Nonemptiness is needed: the empty set has no least element.
/-- If some concept is consistent with `Q` and `N`, the concept generated by `Q` is the least
consistent concept: it is the least general generalization of the objects of `Q`. For a corpus
of terms it is the attribute concept of the least general generalization of the terms of `Q`
(`MathlibLattice.Query.ofObjects_isInstance_eq_ofAttribute`). -/
theorem isLeast_ofObjects (h : (consistentConcepts r Q N).Nonempty) :
    IsLeast (consistentConcepts r Q N) (Concept.ofObjects r Q) :=
  VersionSpace.isLeast_ofObjects_versionSpace h

/-- The consistent concepts are the concepts above the concept generated by `Q` whose extent
contains no object of `N`: a concept has every object of `Q` in its extent exactly when it lies
above the concept generated by `Q`. No chain condition and no nonemptiness are needed. -/
theorem consistentConcepts_eq_Ici_inter (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) :
    consistentConcepts r Q N = Set.Ici (Concept.ofObjects r Q) ∩ {c | Disjoint c.extent N} :=
  VersionSpace.versionSpace_eq_Ici_inter_of_galoisConnection (VersionSpace.gc_ofObjects_extent r)

/-- The answers read on intents: a concept is consistent with `Q` and `N` if and only if its
intent consists of attributes common to the objects of `Q`, and no object `n` of `N` has every
attribute of its intent. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_iff_intent :
    c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N ↔
      c.intent ⊆ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ¬c.intent ⊆ upperPolar r {n} := by
  simp [VersionSpace.versionSpace, subset_upperPolar_iff_subset_lowerPolar, Set.disjoint_right]

/-- The consistent concepts are order-convex: every concept lying between two consistent concepts
is consistent (Mitchell 1982). -/
theorem ordConnected_consistentConcepts (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) :
    (consistentConcepts r Q N).OrdConnected :=
  VersionSpace.versionSpace_extent_ordConnected r Q N

/-- The maximal consistent concepts form an antichain. -/
theorem isAntichain_maximalConsistentConcepts (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) :
    IsAntichain (· ≤ ·) (maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N) :=
  VersionSpace.isAntichain_maximal_versionSpace _ Q N

-- The ascending chain condition is needed: for the objects `WithTop ℕ`, the attributes `ℕ`, the
-- incidence `a ≤ ↑b`, `Q = ∅` and `N = {⊤}`, the consistent concepts are the concepts with
-- extent `Set.Iic ↑n` for `n : ℕ`, and none of them is maximal.
/-- If the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, in particular if there are finitely
many objects or finitely many attributes, every consistent concept lies below a maximal
consistent concept. -/
theorem exists_mem_maximalConsistentConcepts_le [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)]
    (hc : c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N) : ∃ m ∈ maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N, c ≤ m :=
  (VersionSpace.exists_le_maximal_versionSpace hc).imp fun _ h => ⟨h.2, h.1⟩

-- The ascending chain condition is needed, by the example before
-- `exists_mem_maximalConsistentConcepts_le`: there the left side is nonempty and the right side
-- is empty.
/-- Boundary representation of the answers (Mitchell 1982): if the concept lattice has no infinite
increasing chain, in particular for a finite context, the concepts consistent with `Q` and `N`
are exactly the concepts lying between the concept generated by `Q` and some maximal consistent
concept. -/
theorem consistentConcepts_eq_biUnion_Icc [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)] :
    consistentConcepts r Q N =
      ⋃ m ∈ maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N, Set.Icc (Concept.ofObjects r Q) m := by
  ext c
  simp only [Set.mem_iUnion, Set.mem_Icc, exists_prop]
  exact ⟨fun hc => (exists_mem_maximalConsistentConcepts_le hc).imp fun _ hm =>
      ⟨hm.1, (isLeast_ofObjects ⟨c, hc⟩).2 hc, hm.2⟩,
    fun ⟨m, hm, hmc⟩ => (ordConnected_consistentConcepts r Q N).out
      (isLeast_ofObjects ⟨m, hm.prop⟩).1 hm.prop hmc⟩

-- Nonemptiness is needed: for the objects `Bool`, one attribute held by `true` alone, and
-- `Q = N = {true}`, no concept is consistent, so every object is a certain prediction, while the
-- concept generated by `Q` has extent `{true}`.
/-- If some concept is consistent with `Q` and `N`, the certain predictions of the query are the
objects in the extent of the concept generated by `Q`. -/
theorem certainPredictions_eq (h : (consistentConcepts r Q N).Nonempty) :
    certainPredictions r Q N = (Concept.ofObjects r Q).extent :=
  VersionSpace.certainPredictions_eq_of_isLeast Concept.strictMono_extent.monotone
    (isLeast_ofObjects h)

-- The ascending chain condition is needed, by the example before
-- `exists_mem_maximalConsistentConcepts_le`: there the possible predictions are the objects
-- `↑n` for `n : ℕ` and the right side is empty.
/-- If the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, in particular for a finite context,
the possible predictions of the query are the objects in the extent of some maximal consistent
concept. -/
theorem possiblePredictions_eq [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)] :
    possiblePredictions r Q N = ⋃ m ∈ maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N, m.extent :=
  VersionSpace.possiblePredictions_eq_maximal Concept.strictMono_extent.monotone
    fun _ hc => VersionSpace.exists_le_maximal_versionSpace hc

/-- More positive objects or more negative objects leave fewer consistent concepts. -/
theorem consistentConcepts_anti (hQ : Q ⊆ Q') (hN : N ⊆ N') :
    consistentConcepts r Q' N' ⊆ consistentConcepts r Q N :=
  VersionSpace.versionSpace_anti hQ hN

/-- More positive objects or more negative objects give more certain predictions. -/
theorem certainPredictions_mono (hQ : Q ⊆ Q') (hN : N ⊆ N') :
    certainPredictions r Q N ⊆ certainPredictions r Q' N' :=
  Set.biInter_subset_biInter_left (consistentConcepts_anti hQ hN)

/-- More positive objects or more negative objects give fewer possible predictions. -/
theorem possiblePredictions_anti (hQ : Q ⊆ Q') (hN : N ⊆ N') :
    possiblePredictions r Q' N' ⊆ possiblePredictions r Q N :=
  Set.biUnion_subset_biUnion_left (consistentConcepts_anti hQ hN)

end Answers

/-! ### Answers that are attribute concepts -/

section AttributeConcepts

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {Q N : Set α} {b : β}

/-- The attribute concept of `b`, the concept of the objects having `b`, is consistent with `Q`
and `N` if and only if every object of `Q` has `b` and no object of `N` has `b`. -/
theorem ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff :
    Concept.ofAttribute r b ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N ↔
      b ∈ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ¬r n b := by
  simp [VersionSpace.versionSpace, Set.subset_def, Set.disjoint_right, mem_upperPolar_iff]

/-- The answers are the attribute concepts of the attributes common to the objects of `Q` that no
object of `N` has if and only if every answer is an attribute concept. These attribute concepts are
always answers (`ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff`), so no hypothesis on the context is
needed. In the context of a corpus, a least upper bound of the terms of the extent of every answer
is one sufficient condition
(`MathlibLattice.Query.consistentConcepts_eq_image_ofAttribute_of_isLUB`). -/
theorem consistentConcepts_eq_image_ofAttribute_iff :
    consistentConcepts r Q N =
        Concept.ofAttribute r '' {b | b ∈ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ¬r n b} ↔
      consistentConcepts r Q N ⊆ Set.range (Concept.ofAttribute r) := by
  grind [ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff]

-- The hypothesis `h` cannot be dropped: for the objects `Fin 4` and two attributes, one held by
-- every object but `3` and one held by every object but `2`, `Q = {0, 1}` and `N = {2, 3}`, the
-- concept generated by `Q` is the only consistent concept and is no attribute concept, so the right
-- side is empty. If the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, it can be weakened to
-- every most general answer being an attribute concept
-- (`maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_iff_subset_range_ofAttribute`).
/-- If every answer is an attribute concept, the most general answers are the maximal attribute
concepts of the attributes common to the objects of `Q` that no object of `N` has. -/
theorem maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_subset_range_ofAttribute
    (h : consistentConcepts r Q N ⊆ Set.range (Concept.ofAttribute r)) :
    maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N =
      {c | Maximal (· ∈ Concept.ofAttribute r ''
        {b | b ∈ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ¬r n b}) c} :=
  congrArg (fun s : Set (Concept α β r) => {c | Maximal (· ∈ s) c})
    (consistentConcepts_eq_image_ofAttribute_iff.2 h)

-- The ascending chain condition is needed: take the objects `ℕ` and two more objects `x` and `y`,
-- an attribute held by `0` alone and, for each `i ≥ 1`, an attribute held by `0, …, i` and `x` and
-- one held by `0, …, i` and `y`, with `Q = ∅` and `N = {x, y}`. The consistent extents are the sets
-- `{0, …, i}`, so no consistent concept is maximal and the right side holds, while the concept with
-- extent `{0}` is maximal among the consistent attribute concepts.
/-- If the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, in particular for a finite context,
the most general answers are the maximal attribute concepts of the attributes common to the
objects of `Q` that no object of `N` has if and only if every most general answer is an attribute
concept. -/
theorem maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_iff_subset_range_ofAttribute
    [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)] :
    maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N =
        {c | Maximal (· ∈ Concept.ofAttribute r ''
          {b | b ∈ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ¬r n b}) c} ↔
      maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N ⊆ Set.range (Concept.ofAttribute r) :=
  ⟨fun h _ hc => (h.subset hc).prop.imp fun _ hb => hb.2,
    fun h => Set.ext fun _ => maximal_iff_maximal_of_imp_of_forall
      (fun _ ⟨_, hb, hc⟩ => hc ▸ ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff.2 hb)
      fun _ hc => (exists_mem_maximalConsistentConcepts_le hc).imp fun _ ⟨hm, hcm⟩ =>
        ⟨hcm, (h hm).elim fun b hb =>
          ⟨b, ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff.1 (hb ▸ hm.prop), hb⟩⟩⟩

/-- The concept generated by `Q`, the least answer when there is one, is the attribute concept of
`b` if and only if every object of `Q` has `b` and every object having `b` lies in the extent of
the concept generated by `Q`. In the context of a corpus a least upper bound of the terms of `Q` is
one such attribute (`MathlibLattice.Query.ofObjects_eq_ofAttribute_of_isLUB`). -/
theorem ofObjects_eq_ofAttribute_iff :
    Concept.ofObjects r Q = Concept.ofAttribute r b ↔
      b ∈ upperPolar r Q ∧ lowerPolar r {b} ⊆ (Concept.ofObjects r Q).extent :=
  le_antisymm_iff.trans (Concept.le_ofAttribute_iff.and Concept.extent_subset_extent_iff.symm)

end AttributeConcepts

/-! ### The most general answers -/

section MostGeneral

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {Q N : Set α}

/-- The sets of attributes separating the positive objects `Q` from the negative objects `N`:
the sets `T` of attributes common to the objects of `Q` such that every object of `N` lacks some
attribute of `T`. Equivalently, `T ⊆ upperPolar r Q` meets `upperPolar r Q \ upperPolar r {n}`
for every `n ∈ N`: `T` is a transversal of these sets. -/
def separatingSets (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) : Set (Set β) :=
  {T | T ⊆ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ∃ b ∈ T, ¬r n b}

/-- A set of attributes separates `Q` from `N` if and only if its attributes are common to the
objects of `Q` and every object of `N` lacks one of them. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_separatingSets {T : Set β} :
    T ∈ separatingSets r Q N ↔ T ⊆ upperPolar r Q ∧ ∀ n ∈ N, ∃ b ∈ T, ¬r n b :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- The intent of a consistent concept separates `Q` from `N`. -/
private lemma intent_mem_separatingSets {c : Concept α β r} (hc : c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N) :
    c.intent ∈ separatingSets r Q N := by
  simpa [Set.not_subset] using mem_consistentConcepts_iff_intent.1 hc

/-- The concept generated by a set of attributes separating `Q` from `N` is consistent. -/
private lemma ofAttributes_mem_consistentConcepts {T : Set β} (hT : T ∈ separatingSets r Q N) :
    Concept.ofAttributes r T ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N :=
  ⟨subset_upperPolar_iff_subset_lowerPolar.1 hT.1, Set.disjoint_right.2 fun n hn hnT =>
    (hT.2 n hn).elim fun _ ⟨hb, hnb⟩ => hnb (hnT hb)⟩

/-- The consistent concepts are the concepts generated by the sets of attributes separating `Q`
from `N`: the concept generated by a separating set is consistent, and the intent of a consistent
concept is a separating set (`mem_consistentConcepts_iff_intent`). No chain condition is
needed. -/
theorem consistentConcepts_eq_image_ofAttributes (r : α → β → Prop) (Q N : Set α) :
    consistentConcepts r Q N = Concept.ofAttributes r '' separatingSets r Q N :=
  Set.Subset.antisymm
    (fun c hc => ⟨c.intent, intent_mem_separatingSets hc, Concept.ofAttributes_intent⟩)
    (Set.image_subset_iff.2 fun _ => ofAttributes_mem_consistentConcepts)

-- The hypothesis `h` is needed: for the objects `ℕ`, the attributes `ℕ`, the incidence `b < n`,
-- `Q = ∅` and `N = Set.univ`, the separating sets are the unbounded sets, none of which is
-- minimal, while the concept with empty extent is a maximal consistent concept.
/-- The most general answers come from the minimal separating sets (Mitchell's general boundary,
Mitchell 1982): if every set of attributes separating `Q` from `N` contains a minimal one, the
maximal consistent concepts are the maximal concepts among those generated by the minimal sets of
attributes separating `Q` from `N`. -/
theorem maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal
    (h : ∀ T ∈ separatingSets r Q N, ∃ T₀ ⊆ T, Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T₀) :
    maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N =
      {c | Maximal (· ∈ Concept.ofAttributes r ''
        {T | Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T}) c} :=
  Set.ext fun _ => maximal_iff_maximal_of_imp_of_forall
    (fun _ ⟨_, hT, hc⟩ => hc ▸ ofAttributes_mem_consistentConcepts hT.prop)
    fun c hc => (h c.intent (intent_mem_separatingSets hc)).imp' _ fun T₀ ⟨hT₀, hmin⟩ =>
      ⟨Concept.le_ofAttributes_iff.2 hT₀, T₀, hmin, rfl⟩

-- Finiteness is needed here and in the next theorem: in the example before
-- `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal` there are infinitely many attributes
-- and infinitely many negative objects.
/-- For finitely many attributes, the maximal consistent concepts are the maximal concepts among
those generated by the minimal sets of attributes separating `Q` from `N`. -/
theorem maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_finite [Finite β] :
    maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N =
      {c | Maximal (· ∈ Concept.ofAttributes r ''
        {T | Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T}) c} :=
  maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal fun _ hT =>
    (Set.toFinite _).exists_le_minimal hT

/-- A finite separating set contains a minimal separating set. -/
private lemma exists_subset_minimal_of_finite {T : Set β} (hT : T.Finite)
    (hsep : T ∈ separatingSets r Q N) : ∃ T₀ ⊆ T, Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T₀ :=
  (hT.finite_subsets.inter_of_left _).exists_le_minimal ⟨show T ⊆ T from subset_rfl, hsep⟩ |>.imp
    fun _ ⟨hT₀, hmin⟩ => ⟨hT₀, hmin.prop.2, fun _ ht htT₀ => hmin.2 ⟨htT₀.trans hT₀, ht⟩ htT₀⟩

/-- For finitely many negative objects, every separating set contains a finite separating set. -/
private lemma exists_finite_subset_mem_separatingSets (hN : N.Finite) {T : Set β}
    (hT : T ∈ separatingSets r Q N) : ∃ T' ⊆ T, T'.Finite ∧ T' ∈ separatingSets r Q N := by
  choose f hfT hf using hT.2
  refine ⟨{b | ∃ n, ∃ hn : n ∈ N, f n hn = b}, ?_, hN.dependent_image f, ?_, fun n hn =>
    ⟨f n hn, ⟨n, hn, rfl⟩, hf n hn⟩⟩
  · rintro _ ⟨n, hn, rfl⟩
    exact hfT n hn
  · rintro _ ⟨n, hn, rfl⟩
    exact hT.1 (hfT n hn)

/-- For finitely many negative objects, the maximal consistent concepts are the maximal concepts
among those generated by the minimal sets of attributes separating `Q` from `N`: every separating
set contains a finite one, with one attribute for each negative object, and hence a minimal
one. -/
theorem maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_finite_negatives (hN : N.Finite) :
    maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N =
      {c | Maximal (· ∈ Concept.ofAttributes r ''
        {T | Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T}) c} :=
  maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal fun _ hT =>
    (exists_finite_subset_mem_separatingSets hN hT).elim fun _ ⟨hT'T, hT', hsep⟩ =>
      (exists_subset_minimal_of_finite hT' hsep).imp fun _ ⟨h₀, hmin⟩ => ⟨h₀.trans hT'T, hmin⟩

-- The hypothesis `h` is needed, by the example before
-- `maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal`: there the left side is `1` and the
-- right side is `0`.
/-- If every set of attributes separating `Q` from `N` contains a minimal one, there are at most
as many maximal consistent concepts as minimal separating sets. -/
theorem encard_maximalConsistentConcepts_le
    (h : ∀ T ∈ separatingSets r Q N, ∃ T₀ ⊆ T, Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T₀) :
    (maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N).encard ≤
      {T | Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T}.encard := by
  rw [maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_forall_exists_minimal h]
  exact (Set.encard_le_encard fun _ hc => hc.prop).trans (Set.encard_image_le _ _)

-- `Finite β` is needed: for a single object `n` with no attribute, infinitely many attributes,
-- `Q = ∅` and `N = {n}`, there is one maximal consistent concept and there are infinitely many
-- minimal separating sets, so the right side is `0`.
/-- For finitely many attributes, there are at most as many maximal consistent concepts as minimal
sets of attributes separating `Q` from `N`. -/
theorem card_maximalConsistentConcepts_le [Finite β] :
    Nat.card (maximalConsistentConcepts r Q N) ≤
      Nat.card {T | Minimal (· ∈ separatingSets r Q N) T} := by
  rw [maximalConsistentConcepts_eq_of_finite, Nat.card_coe_set_eq, Nat.card_coe_set_eq]
  exact (Set.ncard_le_ncard fun _ hc => hc.prop).trans (Set.ncard_image_le (Set.toFinite _))

/-- For a single negative object `n`, the most general answers are the maximal attribute concepts
of the attributes common to the objects of `Q` that `n` lacks: the minimal separating sets are
the singletons of these attributes. -/
theorem maximalConsistentConcepts_singleton (n : α) :
    maximalConsistentConcepts r Q {n} =
      {c | Maximal (· ∈ Concept.ofAttribute r '' {b | b ∈ upperPolar r Q ∧ ¬r n b}) c} :=
  Set.ext fun _ => maximal_iff_maximal_of_imp_of_forall
    (fun _ ⟨_, hb, hc⟩ => hc ▸ ofAttribute_mem_consistentConcepts_iff.2
      ⟨hb.1, fun _ hn => Set.mem_singleton_iff.1 hn ▸ hb.2⟩)
    fun _ hc => ((intent_mem_separatingSets hc).2 n rfl).imp' _ fun b ⟨hb, hnb⟩ =>
      ⟨Concept.le_ofAttribute_iff.2 hb, b, ⟨(intent_mem_separatingSets hc).1 hb, hnb⟩, rfl⟩

end MostGeneral

/-! ### Equivariance -/

section Action

open scoped Pointwise

variable {G : Type*} [Group G] [MulAction G α] [MulAction G β] {r : α → β → Prop}
  [Transform.IsInvariantContext G r]

/-- The query is equivariant: for a group acting on the objects and the attributes of an
invariant context, translating the positive and the negative objects by `g` translates the
consistent concepts by `g`. -/
theorem consistentConcepts_smul (g : G) (Q N : Set α) :
    consistentConcepts r (g • Q) (g • N) = g • consistentConcepts r Q N :=
  (Transform.smul_versionSpace (fun _ => congrFun rfl) g Q N).symm

end Action

/-! ### Unstated concepts, mass and proper joins -/

section Unstated

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {c : Concept α β r}

/-- A concept is *unstated* if it is not the object concept of any object, that is, no single
object generates it. -/
def IsUnstated (r : α → β → Prop) (c : Concept α β r) : Prop :=
  c ∉ Set.range (Concept.ofObject r)

-- `Finite α` is needed: for infinite `α` every mass is `0`, while the object concepts are not
-- unstated.
/-- In a context with finitely many objects, a concept is unstated if and only if its mass, the
share of the objects whose object concept it is, is zero. -/
theorem isUnstated_iff_mass_eq_zero [Finite α] : IsUnstated r c ↔ Concept.mass r c = 0 :=
  Concept.mass_eq_zero_iff.symm

/-- An unstated concept is a proper join: it is the least upper bound of the concepts strictly
below it, that is, it is not completely sup-irreducible. Indeed the object concepts are
join-dense, so every completely sup-irreducible concept is an object concept. -/
theorem IsUnstated.not_completelySupIrred (hc : IsUnstated r c) : ¬CompletelySupIrred c :=
  fun h => hc (h.mem_range_of_supDense Concept.supDense_ofObject)

/-- The unstated concepts are exactly the proper joins, the concepts that are not completely
sup-irreducible, if and only if every object concept is completely sup-irreducible. -/
theorem forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred :
    (∀ c : Concept α β r, IsUnstated r c ↔ ¬CompletelySupIrred c) ↔
      ∀ a, CompletelySupIrred (Concept.ofObject r a) :=
  ⟨fun H a => not_not.1 fun h => (H _).2 h ⟨a, rfl⟩,
    fun H _ => ⟨IsUnstated.not_completelySupIrred, fun h ⟨a, ha⟩ => h (ha ▸ H a)⟩⟩

-- Reducedness is needed: the context of `not_forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred` is not
-- reduced, and the equivalence fails there.
/-- In a reduced context a concept is unstated if and only if it is a proper join, that is, if
and only if it is not completely sup-irreducible (Ganter–Wille 1999). -/
theorem isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred_of_isReduced (h : Order.IsReduced r)
    (c : Concept α β r) : IsUnstated r c ↔ ¬CompletelySupIrred c :=
  forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred.2 (Order.isReduced_iff.1 h).2.1 c

-- `Finite α` is needed for the mass, as for `isUnstated_iff_mass_eq_zero`. The hypothesis `h` is
-- needed for the proper joins: `forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred` shows that the
-- equivalence of the first and third statements for every `c` implies it, and
-- `not_forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred` gives a context where it fails.
/-- If there are finitely many objects and every object concept is completely sup-irreducible, as
in a finite reduced context, the following are equivalent for a concept `c`: `c` is unstated, the
mass of `c` is zero, and `c` is a proper join, the least upper bound of the concepts strictly
below it. -/
theorem tfae_isUnstated [Finite α] (h : ∀ a, CompletelySupIrred (Concept.ofObject r a))
    (c : Concept α β r) :
    List.TFAE [IsUnstated r c, Concept.mass r c = 0, ¬CompletelySupIrred c] := by
  tfae_have 1 ↔ 2 := isUnstated_iff_mass_eq_zero
  tfae_have 1 ↔ 3 := forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred.2 h c
  tfae_finish

/-- For `b = 0 ∨ b = a` on `Fin 3`, the object concept of `a ≠ 0` is strictly below that of `0`. -/
private lemma ofObject_lt_ofObject_zero {a : Fin 3} (ha : a ≠ 0) :
    Concept.ofObject (fun a b : Fin 3 => b = 0 ∨ b = a) a < Concept.ofObject _ 0 :=
  lt_of_le_not_ge (by simp [mem_lowerPolar_iff, mem_upperPolar_iff])
    (by simpa [Concept.ofObject_le_iff, mem_lowerPolar_iff, mem_upperPolar_iff] using ha)

/-- For `b = 0 ∨ b = a` on `Fin 3`, `Concept.ofObject _ 0` is the join of those of `1` and `2`. -/
private lemma ofObject_zero_eq_sup :
    Concept.ofObject (fun a b : Fin 3 => b = 0 ∨ b = a) 0 =
      Concept.ofObject _ 1 ⊔ Concept.ofObject _ 2 :=
  Concept.ext' (Set.ext fun _ => by simp [mem_upperPolar_iff]; grind)

/-- A consistent concept can be stated and a proper join at once, so being unstated is not
equivalent to being a proper join. In the context on `Fin 3` in which the object `a` has the
attributes `0` and `a`, the concept generated by the objects `1` and `2` is consistent with
`Q = {1, 2}` and `N = ∅`. It is the object concept of `0`, and it is the join of the object
concepts of `1` and `2`, which lie strictly below it. -/
theorem not_forall_isUnstated_iff_not_completelySupIrred :
    ¬∀ c ∈ consistentConcepts (fun a b : Fin 3 => b = 0 ∨ b = a) {1, 2} ∅,
      IsUnstated _ c ↔ ¬CompletelySupIrred c :=
  fun h => (h _ ⟨Set.insert_subset_iff.2 ⟨Concept.ofObject_le_iff.1
      (ofObject_lt_ofObject_zero (by decide)).le, Set.singleton_subset_iff.2
        (Concept.ofObject_le_iff.1 (ofObject_lt_ofObject_zero (by decide)).le)⟩,
      Set.disjoint_empty _⟩).2
    (fun hirr => hirr ⟨fun _ hc => hc.le, fun _ hu => ofObject_zero_eq_sup.le.trans
      (sup_le (hu (ofObject_lt_ofObject_zero (by decide)))
        (hu (ofObject_lt_ofObject_zero (by decide))))⟩) ⟨0, rfl⟩

end Unstated

/-! ### Normal forms -/

section NormalForm

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {c c' m : Concept α β r}

/-- The normal form of a concept `c`: the set of completely inf-irreducible concepts above `c`
(Ganter–Wille 1999). -/
def normalForm (r : α → β → Prop) (c : Concept α β r) : Set (Concept α β r) :=
  {m | CompletelyInfIrred m ∧ c ≤ m}

/-- A concept lies in the normal form of `c` if and only if it is completely inf-irreducible and
lies above `c`. -/
@[simp]
theorem mem_normalForm : m ∈ normalForm r c ↔ CompletelyInfIrred m ∧ c ≤ m :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- Every member of the normal form of `c` is the attribute concept of an attribute in the intent
of `c`: the normal form is given by descriptions of `c`. No chain condition is needed: the
attribute concepts are meet-dense, and every completely inf-irreducible concept lies in the range
of every meet-dense family. -/
theorem exists_mem_intent_ofAttribute_eq (hm : m ∈ normalForm r c) :
    ∃ b ∈ c.intent, Concept.ofAttribute r b = m :=
  (hm.1.mem_range_of_infDense Concept.infDense_ofAttribute).imp
    fun b (hb : Concept.ofAttribute r b = m) => ⟨Concept.le_ofAttribute_iff.1 (hb ▸ hm.2), hb⟩

/-- The normal form of `c` consists of the attribute concepts of the attributes in the intent of
`c` that are completely inf-irreducible. -/
theorem normalForm_eq_image_ofAttribute (c : Concept α β r) :
    normalForm r c = Concept.ofAttribute r ''
      {b | b ∈ c.intent ∧ CompletelyInfIrred (Concept.ofAttribute r b)} := by
  ext m
  constructor
  · intro hm
    obtain ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩ := exists_mem_intent_ofAttribute_eq hm
    exact ⟨b, ⟨hb, hm.1⟩, rfl⟩
  · rintro ⟨b, ⟨hb, hirr⟩, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨hirr, Concept.le_ofAttribute_iff.2 hb⟩

/-- The normal form of `c` is the image of the completely inf-irreducible concepts above `c`. -/
private lemma normalForm_eq_image_val (c : Concept α β r) :
    normalForm r c = Subtype.val '' {m : {m : Concept α β r // CompletelyInfIrred m} | c ≤ m} := by
  aesop

/-- Every concept is the meet of its normal form if and only if the completely inf-irreducible
concepts are meet-dense. -/
theorem forall_sInf_normalForm_eq_iff :
    (∀ c : Concept α β r, sInf (normalForm r c) = c) ↔
      InfDense ((↑) : {m : Concept α β r // CompletelyInfIrred m} → Concept α β r) := by
  simp only [InfDense, isGLB_iff_sInf_eq, normalForm_eq_image_val]

-- The hypothesis is needed: by `forall_sInf_normalForm_eq_iff` it is equivalent to the
-- conclusion for every `c`.
/-- If the completely inf-irreducible concepts are meet-dense, every concept, in particular every
answer to the query, is the meet of its normal form (Ganter–Wille 1999). -/
theorem sInf_normalForm
    (h : InfDense ((↑) : {m : Concept α β r // CompletelyInfIrred m} → Concept α β r))
    (c : Concept α β r) : sInf (normalForm r c) = c :=
  forall_sInf_normalForm_eq_iff.2 h c

/-- The meet of the normal form of `c` has the normal form of `c`. -/
private lemma normalForm_sInf_normalForm (c : Concept α β r) :
    normalForm r (sInf (normalForm r c)) = normalForm r c :=
  Set.ext fun _ => ⟨fun hm => ⟨hm.1, (le_sInf fun _ hm' => hm'.2).trans hm.2⟩,
    fun hm => ⟨hm.1, sInf_le hm⟩⟩

/-- The normal form determines the concept if and only if the completely inf-irreducible
concepts are meet-dense: otherwise some `c` lies strictly below the meet of its normal form, and
that meet has the same normal form. -/
theorem injective_normalForm_iff :
    Function.Injective (normalForm r) ↔
      InfDense ((↑) : {m : Concept α β r // CompletelyInfIrred m} → Concept α β r) :=
  ⟨fun h => forall_sInf_normalForm_eq_iff.1 fun c => h (normalForm_sInf_normalForm c),
    fun h _ _ hc => (sInf_normalForm h _).symm.trans
      ((congrArg sInf hc).trans (sInf_normalForm h _))⟩

-- The hypothesis is needed here and in the next theorem, by `injective_normalForm_iff`.
/-- Normal forms are unique: if the completely inf-irreducible concepts are meet-dense, two
concepts with the same normal form are equal. -/
theorem normalForm_inj
    (h : InfDense ((↑) : {m : Concept α β r // CompletelyInfIrred m} → Concept α β r)) :
    normalForm r c = normalForm r c' ↔ c = c' :=
  ⟨fun hc => (sInf_normalForm h c).symm.trans ((congrArg sInf hc).trans (sInf_normalForm h c')),
    congrArg _⟩

/-- If the completely inf-irreducible concepts are meet-dense, the normal form reverses the
order: the normal form of `c'` is contained in that of `c` if and only if `c ≤ c'`. -/
theorem normalForm_subset_normalForm_iff
    (h : InfDense ((↑) : {m : Concept α β r // CompletelyInfIrred m} → Concept α β r)) :
    normalForm r c' ⊆ normalForm r c ↔ c ≤ c' :=
  ⟨fun hc => (sInf_normalForm h c).ge.trans ((sInf_le_sInf hc).trans (sInf_normalForm h c').le),
    fun hc _ hm => ⟨hm.1, hc.trans hm.2⟩⟩

-- The ascending chain condition is needed here and in the next two theorems: a nontrivial
-- complete atomless Boolean algebra, isomorphic to the concept lattice of its standard context
-- (`DedekindCut.principalIso`), has no completely inf-irreducible element, so every normal form is
-- empty.
/-- If the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, in particular for a finite context,
every concept, in particular every answer to the query, is the meet of its normal form
(Ganter–Wille 1999). -/
theorem sInf_normalForm_of_wellFoundedGT [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)] (c : Concept α β r) :
    sInf (normalForm r c) = c :=
  sInf_normalForm infDense_completelyInfIrred c

/-- Normal forms are unique: if the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, two
concepts with the same normal form are equal. -/
theorem normalForm_inj_of_wellFoundedGT [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)] :
    normalForm r c = normalForm r c' ↔ c = c' :=
  normalForm_inj infDense_completelyInfIrred

/-- If the concept lattice has no infinite increasing chain, the normal form reverses the order:
the normal form of `c'` is contained in that of `c` if and only if `c ≤ c'`. -/
theorem normalForm_subset_normalForm_iff_of_wellFoundedGT [WellFoundedGT (Concept α β r)] :
    normalForm r c' ⊆ normalForm r c ↔ c ≤ c' :=
  normalForm_subset_normalForm_iff infDense_completelyInfIrred

-- The descending chain condition is needed: the context `(ℕ, ℕ, fun a b => b ≤ a)` has the
-- extents `∅` and `Set.Ici n`, and its concept with empty extent is inf-irreducible but is the
-- meet of the concepts above it, so it is not completely inf-irreducible.
/-- If the concept lattice has no infinite decreasing chain, in particular for a finite context,
the normal form of `c` is the set of inf-irreducible concepts above `c`, since inf-irreducible
and completely inf-irreducible concepts agree (`infIrred_iff_completelyInfIrred`). -/
theorem normalForm_eq_setOf_infIrred [WellFoundedLT (Concept α β r)] (c : Concept α β r) :
    normalForm r c = {m | InfIrred m ∧ c ≤ m} :=
  Set.ext fun _ => and_congr_left' (infIrred_iff_completelyInfIrred fun _ h => h.isTop).symm

end NormalForm

/-! ### Finer levels of description -/

section Refinement

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {r' : α → β' → Prop}

/-- Every extent of `r` is an extent of `r'`, so that `r'` describes the same objects at least as
finely as `r`, if and only if, for every set of objects `Q`, the extent of the concept of `r'`
generated by `Q` is contained in the extent of the concept of `r` generated by `Q`. This is
`MathlibLattice.Height.forall_isExtent_imp_iff` for the pattern structures of attribute sets. -/
theorem forall_isExtent_imp_iff :
    (∀ A, Order.IsExtent r A → Order.IsExtent r' A) ↔
      ∀ Q : Set α, (Concept.ofObjects r' Q).extent ⊆ (Concept.ofObjects r Q).extent :=
  (Height.forall_isExtent_imp_iff (δ := fun a => {b | r' a b})
      (δ' := fun a => {b | r a b})).trans
    (by simp only [PatternStructure.descr_setOf]; exact Iff.rfl)

-- The hypothesis is needed: by `forall_isExtent_imp_iff` it is equivalent to the conclusion for
-- every `Q`.
/-- At a finer level of description the least general generalization is more specific: if every
extent of `r` is an extent of `r'`, the extent of the concept of `r'` generated by `Q` is
contained in the extent of the concept of `r` generated by `Q`. -/
theorem extent_ofObjects_subset (h : ∀ A, Order.IsExtent r A → Order.IsExtent r' A)
    (Q : Set α) : (Concept.ofObjects r' Q).extent ⊆ (Concept.ofObjects r Q).extent :=
  forall_isExtent_imp_iff.1 h Q

-- The hypothesis is needed: for an extent `A` of `r`, `Q = A` and `N = Aᶜ`, the only consistent
-- extent of `r` is `A`, so the conclusion says that `A` is an extent of `r'`.
/-- At a finer level of description every answer stays an answer: if every extent of `r` is an
extent of `r'`, the extent of every concept of `r` consistent with `Q` and `N` is the extent of a
concept of `r'` consistent with `Q` and `N`. -/
theorem extent_image_consistentConcepts_subset
    (h : ∀ A, Order.IsExtent r A → Order.IsExtent r' A) (Q N : Set α) :
    Concept.extent '' consistentConcepts r Q N ⊆ Concept.extent '' consistentConcepts r' Q N :=
  fun _ ⟨c, hc, he⟩ => ⟨Concept.ofIsExtent r' c.extent (h _ c.isExtent_extent), hc, he⟩

/-- If the objects having each attribute of `R` form an extent of `R'`, so does every extent. -/
private lemma isExtent_of_forall_isExtent {γ γ' : Type*} {R : α → γ → Prop} {R' : α → γ' → Prop}
    (h : ∀ d, Order.IsExtent R' {a | R a d}) {A : Set α} (hA : Order.IsExtent R A) :
    Order.IsExtent R' A := by
  obtain ⟨T, rfl⟩ := hA
  rw [← Set.biUnion_of_singleton T, lowerPolar_iUnion₂]
  exact .iInter₂ _ fun d _ => (Set.ext fun _ => (mem_lowerPolar_singleton R).symm) ▸ h d

/-- Every extent of a pattern structure is an extent of its representation context. -/
private lemma order_isExtent_of_isExtent [LE D] {δ : α → D} {A : Set α}
    (hA : PatternStructure.IsExtent δ A) : Order.IsExtent (fun a d => d ≤ δ a) A := by
  obtain ⟨d, rfl⟩ := hA
  exact ⟨{d}, Set.ext fun _ => mem_lowerPolar_singleton _⟩

-- The chain condition `hψ` is needed, as for `MathlibLattice.Height.isExtent_level_of_le`: for
-- `δ a = {a}` in `Set (Fin 2)`, `ψ 0 = id` and `ψ k` the projection to `⊥` for `k ≥ 1`, the
-- extent `{0}` of level `0` is not an extent of level `1`. The hypothesis `hk` is needed: for the
-- same `δ`, `ψ 0` the projection to `⊥` and `ψ k = id` for `k ≥ 1`, the extent `{0}` of level `1`
-- is not an extent of level `0`.
/-- Extents increase with the level: if `ψ k d ≤ ψ (k + 1) d` for every `k` and `d`, and `k ≤ k'`,
every extent of the formal context of level `k`, which describes each object `a` by
`MathlibLattice.Height.level ψ δ k a`, is an extent of the formal context of level `k'`. For a
complete meet-semilattice of descriptions these extents are the extents of the pattern structures
of the levels (`PatternStructure.isExtent_iff_order_isExtent`), and the statement is
`MathlibLattice.Height.isExtent_level_of_le`. -/
theorem order_isExtent_level_of_le [Preorder D] {ψ : ℕ → PatternStructure.Projection D}
    (hψ : ∀ k d, ψ k d ≤ ψ (k + 1) d) (δ : α → D) {k k' : ℕ} (hk : k ≤ k') (A : Set α)
    (hA : Order.IsExtent (fun a d => d ≤ Height.level ψ δ k a) A) :
    Order.IsExtent (fun a d => d ≤ Height.level ψ δ k' a) A :=
  isExtent_of_forall_isExtent (fun d => order_isExtent_of_isExtent
    (Height.isExtent_level_of_le hψ δ hk _ (PatternStructure.isExtent_extentOf _ d))) hA

-- The hypotheses `hψ` and `hk` are needed, by the examples before `order_isExtent_level_of_le`:
-- in the first the concept generated by `{0}` has extent `{0}` at level `0` and `Set.univ` at
-- level `1`, and in the second the other way round.
/-- At a finer level of the height filtration the least general generalization is more specific:
if `ψ k d ≤ ψ (k + 1) d` for every `k` and `d`, and `k ≤ k'`, the extent of the concept generated
by `Q` in the formal context of level `k'` is contained in its extent at level `k`. The formal
context of level `k` describes each object `a` by `MathlibLattice.Height.level ψ δ k a`; for a
complete meet-semilattice of descriptions its concepts are the pattern concepts of level `k`
(`PatternConcept.orderIsoConcept`), and the statement is
`MathlibLattice.Height.antitone_extentOf_descr_level`. -/
theorem extent_ofObjects_level_subset [Preorder D] {ψ : ℕ → PatternStructure.Projection D}
    (hψ : ∀ k d, ψ k d ≤ ψ (k + 1) d) (δ : α → D) {k k' : ℕ} (hk : k ≤ k') (Q : Set α) :
    (Concept.ofObjects (fun a d => d ≤ Height.level ψ δ k' a) Q).extent ⊆
      (Concept.ofObjects (fun a d => d ≤ Height.level ψ δ k a) Q).extent :=
  extent_ofObjects_subset (order_isExtent_level_of_le hψ δ hk) Q

end Refinement

/-! ### Adding a context of statements -/

section AddContext

variable {α' : Type*} {r : α → β → Prop} {r' : α' → β → Prop} {Q N : Set α} {Q' N' : Set α'}

/-- A concept of `Sum.elim r r'` is consistent iff its intent generates consistent concepts. -/
private lemma mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes
    {P₁ N₁ : Set (α ⊕ α')} {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') P₁ N₁ ↔
      Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent ∈ consistentConcepts r (Sum.inl ⁻¹' P₁) (Sum.inl ⁻¹' N₁) ∧
        Concept.ofAttributes r' c'.intent ∈
          consistentConcepts r' (Sum.inr ⁻¹' P₁) (Sum.inr ⁻¹' N₁) := by
  simp only [VersionSpace.versionSpace, Set.mem_setOf_eq, Concept.extent_ofAttributes]
  rw [← c'.lowerPolar_intent]
  simp [Set.subset_def, Set.disjoint_left, Sum.forall, mem_lowerPolar_iff, and_and_and_comm,
    -Concept.lowerPolar_intent]

/-- `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes` for the objects of `r` and of `r'`. -/
private lemma mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff_ofAttributes
    {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q ∪ Sum.inr '' Q')
        (Sum.inl '' N ∪ Sum.inr '' N') ↔
      Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N ∧
        Concept.ofAttributes r' c'.intent ∈ consistentConcepts r' Q' N' := by
  simp only [mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes, Set.preimage_union,
    Set.preimage_image_eq, Sum.inl_injective, Sum.inr_injective,
    Set.preimage_inr_image_inl, Set.preimage_inl_image_inr, Set.union_empty, Set.empty_union]

/-- The intent of a concept of `Sum.elim r r'` is the meet of the intents it generates. -/
private lemma intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim (c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')) :
    c'.intent =
      (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent).intent ∩ (Concept.ofAttributes r' c'.intent).intent := by
  obtain ⟨B, B', hB, hB', h⟩ := Order.isIntent_sumElim_iff.1 c'.isIntent_intent
  refine (Set.subset_inter (subset_upperPolar_lowerPolar r _)
    (subset_upperPolar_lowerPolar r' _)).antisymm ?_
  rw [h]
  exact Set.inter_subset_inter (hB.upperPolar_lowerPolar_subset Set.inter_subset_left)
    (hB'.upperPolar_lowerPolar_subset Set.inter_subset_right)

/-- The positive objects have every attribute of a subset of the intent of a consistent concept. -/
private lemma subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent {c : Concept α β r}
    (hc : c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N) {T : Set β} (hT : T ⊆ c.intent) : Q ⊆ lowerPolar r T :=
  hc.1.trans (c.lowerPolar_intent ▸ lowerPolar_anti r hT)

/-- The answers after adding a context `r'` of statements with the same descriptions: a concept of
the subposition `Sum.elim r r'` (Ganter–Wille 1999) is consistent with the positive objects `Q` of
`r` and `Q'` of `r'` and the negative objects `N` of `r` and `N'` of `r'` if and only if its intent
is `c.intent ∩ d.intent` for a concept `c` of `r` consistent with `Q` and `N` and a concept `d` of
`r'` consistent with `Q'` and `N'`, such that no object of `N` and no object of `N'` has every
attribute of `c.intent ∩ d.intent`. This refines the subposition principle
`Order.isIntent_sumElim_iff`: for the intent `T` of an answer, take for `c` and `d` the concepts
generated by `T` in `r` and in `r'`. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q ∪ Sum.inr '' Q')
        (Sum.inl '' N ∪ Sum.inr '' N') ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N, ∃ d ∈ consistentConcepts r' Q' N',
        c'.intent = c.intent ∩ d.intent ∧ Disjoint (lowerPolar r (c.intent ∩ d.intent)) N ∧
          Disjoint (lowerPolar r' (c.intent ∩ d.intent)) N' := by
  rw [mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff_ofAttributes]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨hc, hd⟩ => ⟨_, hc, _, hd, intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c', ?_, ?_⟩,
    fun ⟨c, hc, d, hd, h, hN, hN'⟩ => ?_⟩
  · rw [← intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c']
    exact hc.2
  · rw [← intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c']
    exact hd.2
  · rw [h]
    exact ⟨⟨subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent hc Set.inter_subset_left, hN⟩,
      subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent hd Set.inter_subset_right, hN'⟩

/-- The answers after adding statements that are neither positive nor negative: a concept of the
subposition `Sum.elim r r'` is consistent with `Q` and `N` if and only if its intent is
`c.intent ∩ B'` for a concept `c` of `r` consistent with `Q` and `N` and an intent `B'` of `r'`,
such that no object of `N` has every attribute of `c.intent ∩ B'`. This is
`mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff` for `Q' = N' = ∅`, where every concept of `r'` is
consistent. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q) (Sum.inl '' N) ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N, ∃ B', Order.IsIntent r' B' ∧ c'.intent = c.intent ∩ B' ∧
        Disjoint (lowerPolar r (c.intent ∩ B')) N := by
  simp only [mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes,
    Set.preimage_image_eq, Sum.inl_injective, Set.preimage_inr_image_inl]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨hc, _⟩ => ⟨_, hc, _, Concept.isIntent_intent _, intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c', ?_⟩,
    fun ⟨c, hc, B', _, h, hN⟩ => ?_⟩
  · rw [← intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c']
    exact hc.2
  · rw [h]
    exact ⟨⟨subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent hc Set.inter_subset_left, hN⟩,
      Set.empty_subset _, Set.disjoint_empty _⟩

/-- If all objects of `r'` have the intent of `c'`, it is its `r`-closure ∩ `upperPolar r' univ`. -/
private lemma intent_eq_inter_upperPolar_univ_of_sumElim
    {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} (h : Set.univ ⊆ lowerPolar r' c'.intent) :
    c'.intent = (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent).intent ∩ upperPolar r' Set.univ :=
  (intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c').trans
    (by rw [Concept.intent_ofAttributes r', Set.univ_subset_iff.1 h])

/-- The answers after adding positive statements: a concept of the subposition `Sum.elim r r'` is
consistent with the positive objects `Q` and every object of `r'`, and with the negative objects
`N`, if and only if its intent is `c.intent ∩ upperPolar r' Set.univ`, the attributes of the intent
of `c` that every new statement has, for a concept `c` of `r` consistent with `Q` and `N`, and no
object of `N` has every attribute of this intent. This is `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff`
for `Q' = Set.univ` and `N' = ∅`, where the only consistent concept of `r'` is its top concept. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_positive_iff
    {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q ∪ Set.range Sum.inr) (Sum.inl '' N) ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N, c'.intent = c.intent ∩ upperPolar r' Set.univ ∧
        Disjoint (lowerPolar r (c.intent ∩ upperPolar r' Set.univ)) N := by
  simp only [mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes, Set.preimage_union,
    Set.preimage_image_eq, Sum.inl_injective, Set.preimage_inr_image_inl,
    Set.preimage_inl_range_inr, Set.preimage_range, Set.union_empty, Set.empty_union]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨hc, hd⟩ => ⟨_, hc, intent_eq_inter_upperPolar_univ_of_sumElim hd.1, ?_⟩,
    fun ⟨c, hc, h, hN⟩ => ?_⟩
  · rw [← intent_eq_inter_upperPolar_univ_of_sumElim hd.1]
    exact hc.2
  · rw [h]
    exact ⟨⟨subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent hc Set.inter_subset_left, hN⟩,
      subset_upperPolar_iff_subset_lowerPolar.1 Set.inter_subset_right, Set.disjoint_empty _⟩

/-- If no object of `r'` has its attributes, an intent of `Sum.elim r r'` is an intent of `r`. -/
private lemma intent_eq_intent_ofAttributes_of_sumElim
    {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} (h : lowerPolar r' c'.intent = ∅) :
    c'.intent = (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent).intent :=
  (intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c').trans
    (by rw [Concept.intent_ofAttributes r', h, upperPolar_empty, Set.inter_univ])

/-- The answers after adding negative statements: a concept of the subposition `Sum.elim r r'` is
consistent with the positive objects `Q`, and with the negative objects `N` and every object of
`r'`, if and only if it has the intent of a concept `c` of `r` consistent with `Q` and `N` such that
no object of `r'` has every attribute of the intent of `c`: the old answers that no new statement
fits. This is `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff` for `Q' = ∅` and `N' = Set.univ`, where
the only possible consistent concept of `r'` is the one with empty extent and intent `Set.univ`. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_negative_iff
    {c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r')} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q) (Sum.inl '' N ∪ Set.range Sum.inr) ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N, (∀ a', ¬c.intent ⊆ {b | r' a' b}) ∧
        c'.intent = c.intent := by
  simp only [mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes, Set.preimage_union,
    Set.preimage_image_eq, Sum.inl_injective, Set.preimage_inr_image_inl,
    Set.preimage_inl_range_inr, Set.preimage_range, Set.union_empty, Set.empty_union]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨hc, hd⟩ => ⟨_, hc, fun a' ha' => Set.disjoint_left.1 hd.2
      (fun _ hb => ha' (subset_upperPolar_lowerPolar r _ hb)) (Set.mem_univ a'),
    intent_eq_intent_ofAttributes_of_sumElim (Set.disjoint_univ.1 hd.2)⟩,
    fun ⟨c, hc, hS, h⟩ => ?_⟩
  rw [h, Concept.ofAttributes_intent]
  exact ⟨hc, Set.empty_subset _, Set.disjoint_univ.2 (Set.eq_empty_of_forall_notMem hS)⟩

/-- A concept of `Sum.elim r r'` is determined by the concepts its intent generates in each. -/
private lemma injective_ofAttributes_intent_sumElim :
    Function.Injective fun c' : Concept (α ⊕ α') β (Sum.elim r r') =>
      (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent, Concept.ofAttributes r' c'.intent) :=
  fun c' d' h => Concept.ext' ((intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim c').trans
    ((congrArg (fun p => p.1.intent ∩ p.2.intent) h).trans (intent_eq_inter_of_sumElim d').symm))

/-- Adding a context of statements multiplies the number of answers by at most the number of
answers of the added context: by `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff` the answers of the
subposition are the images of some pairs of answers. No finiteness is needed: if one factor is
infinite and the other is not zero, the right side is `⊤`, and if one factor is zero, no concept of
the subposition is consistent. -/
theorem encard_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le :
    (consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q ∪ Sum.inr '' Q')
        (Sum.inl '' N ∪ Sum.inr '' N')).encard ≤
      (consistentConcepts r Q N).encard * (consistentConcepts r' Q' N').encard :=
  (Set.encard_le_encard_of_injOn (fun _ => mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_union_iff_ofAttributes.1)
    injective_ofAttributes_intent_sumElim.injOn).trans_eq Set.encard_prod

/-- Infinitely many consistent concepts of `r` give infinitely many of `Sum.elim r r'`. -/
private lemma infinite_consistentConcepts_sumElim (h : (consistentConcepts r Q N).Infinite) :
    (consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q) (Sum.inl '' N)).Infinite :=
  Set.infinite_of_injOn_mapsTo (f := fun c : Concept α β r => Concept.ofIsIntent
      (Sum.elim r r') c.intent
      (Order.isIntent_sumElim_iff.2 ⟨_, _, c.isIntent_intent, .univ, (Set.inter_univ _).symm⟩))
    (fun _ _ _ _ hcd => Concept.ext' (congrArg Concept.intent hcd :))
    (fun c hc => mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff.2 ⟨c, hc, Set.univ, .univ,
      (Set.inter_univ _).symm, by simpa using hc.2⟩) h

-- `Finite (Concept α' β r')` is needed: see `not_card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le`.
/-- Adding a context `r'` with finitely many concepts, of statements that are neither positive nor
negative, multiplies the number of consistent concepts by at most the number of concepts of `r'`.
This is `encard_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le` for `Q' = N' = ∅`. If infinitely many concepts are
consistent before, the concepts with their intents are consistent after, and both sides are `0`. -/
theorem card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le [Finite (Concept α' β r')] :
    Nat.card (consistentConcepts (Sum.elim r r') (Sum.inl '' Q) (Sum.inl '' N)) ≤
      Nat.card (consistentConcepts r Q N) * Nat.card (Concept α' β r') := by
  rcases (consistentConcepts r Q N).finite_or_infinite with h | h
  · rw [Nat.card_coe_set_eq, Nat.card_coe_set_eq, ← Set.ncard_univ, ← Set.ncard_prod]
    refine Set.ncard_le_ncard_of_injOn _ (fun c' hc' => ⟨?_, Set.mem_univ _⟩)
      injective_ofAttributes_intent_sumElim.injOn (h.prod Set.finite_univ)
    simpa only [Set.preimage_image_eq, Sum.inl_injective] using
      (mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff_ofAttributes.1 hc').1
  · rw [h.card_eq_zero, zero_mul, (infinite_consistentConcepts_sumElim h).card_eq_zero]

/-- The objects `k + 1` of the context `b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k` have pairwise distinct object concepts. -/
private lemma injective_ofObject_succ_counterexample :
    Function.Injective fun k : ℕ => Concept.ofObject (fun k b : ℕ => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k) (k + 1) :=
  fun k _ h => by simpa using Set.ext_iff.1 (congrArg Concept.intent h) (k + 1)

/-- Below, a concept whose extent misses `Sum.inl false` has the attribute `0` in its intent. -/
private lemma zero_mem_intent_counterexample
    {c' : Concept (Bool ⊕ ℕ) ℕ (Sum.elim (fun (a : Bool) (b : ℕ) => a = true ∨ b ≠ 0)
      (fun (k b : ℕ) => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k))} (h : Sum.inl false ∉ c'.extent) : 0 ∈ c'.intent :=
  by_contra fun h0 => h (c'.lowerPolar_intent ▸ fun _ hb => Or.inr fun hb0 => h0 (hb0 ▸ hb))

/-- In the subposition below, an object having the attribute `0` has every attribute. -/
private lemma sumElim_counterexample_of_zero {x : Bool ⊕ ℕ}
    (h : Sum.elim (fun (a : Bool) (b : ℕ) => a = true ∨ b ≠ 0) (fun (k b : ℕ) => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k) x 0)
    (b : ℕ) :
    Sum.elim (fun (a : Bool) (b : ℕ) => a = true ∨ b ≠ 0) (fun (k b : ℕ) => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k) x b := by
  grind

/-- In the subposition below, the only consistent concept is the least one. -/
private lemma consistentConcepts_sumElim_counterexample :
    consistentConcepts (Sum.elim (fun (a : Bool) (b : ℕ) => a = true ∨ b ≠ 0)
      (fun (k b : ℕ) => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k)) (Sum.inl '' {true}) (Sum.inl '' {false}) = {⊥} := by
  ext c'
  rw [Set.mem_singleton_iff]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨-, hN⟩
    exact le_bot_iff.1 (Concept.extent_subset_extent_iff.1 fun _ hx b _ =>
      sumElim_counterexample_of_zero (Concept.rel_extent_intent hx
        (zero_mem_intent_counterexample (Set.disjoint_left.1 hN · ⟨false, rfl, rfl⟩))) b)
  · rintro rfl
    simp [VersionSpace.versionSpace, Concept.extent_bot, Set.subset_def, Set.disjoint_left,
      mem_lowerPolar_iff]

/-- The finiteness of the added context is needed in `card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le`. In the
context on `Bool` in which `true` has every natural number as an attribute and `false` every one
but `0`, one concept is consistent with `Q = {true}` and `N = {false}`. After adding the statements
`k : ℕ`, each with the attributes other than `0` and `k`, a context with infinitely many concepts,
the only consistent concept is the one with intent `Set.univ`, while the right side is `1 * 0`. -/
theorem not_card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le :
    ¬Nat.card (consistentConcepts (Sum.elim (fun (a : Bool) (b : ℕ) => a = true ∨ b ≠ 0)
          (fun (k b : ℕ) => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k)) (Sum.inl '' {true}) (Sum.inl '' {false})) ≤
      Nat.card (consistentConcepts (fun (a : Bool) (b : ℕ) => a = true ∨ b ≠ 0) {true} {false}) *
        Nat.card (Concept ℕ ℕ fun k b => b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ k) := by
  rw [consistentConcepts_sumElim_counterexample, @Nat.card_eq_zero_of_infinite _
    (Infinite.of_injective _ injective_ofObject_succ_counterexample)]
  simp

end AddContext

/-! ### Adding a statement -/

section AddObject

variable {r : α → β → Prop} {Q N : Set α} (S : Set β)

/-- The concept with the intent of the concept `c` of `r` in the context extended by one new
object with attribute set `S`: every intent of `r` stays an intent of the extended context
(`Order.IsIntent.addObject`). -/
def addObjectConcept (c : Concept α β r) : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S) :=
  Concept.ofIsIntent _ c.intent (c.isIntent_intent.addObject S)

/-- The concept `addObjectConcept S c` has the intent of `c`. -/
@[simp]
theorem intent_addObjectConcept (c : Concept α β r) :
    (addObjectConcept S c).intent = c.intent :=
  rfl

/-- An old object lies in the extent of `addObjectConcept S c` if and only if it lies in the
extent of `c`. -/
@[simp]
theorem some_mem_extent_addObjectConcept {c : Concept α β r} {a : α} :
    some a ∈ (addObjectConcept S c).extent ↔ a ∈ c.extent :=
  c.lowerPolar_intent ▸ Iff.rfl

/-- The new object lies in the extent of `addObjectConcept S c` if and only if it has every
attribute in the intent of `c`. -/
@[simp]
theorem none_mem_extent_addObjectConcept {c : Concept α β r} :
    none ∈ (addObjectConcept S c).extent ↔ c.intent ⊆ S :=
  Iff.rfl

/-- A concept with one more object is consistent iff its intent generates consistent concepts. -/
private lemma mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff_ofAttributes {P₁ N₁ : Set (Option α)}
    {c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) P₁ N₁ ↔
      ((none ∈ P₁ → c'.intent ⊆ S) ∧ (c'.intent ⊆ S → none ∉ N₁)) ∧
        Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent ∈ consistentConcepts r (some ⁻¹' P₁) (some ⁻¹' N₁) := by
  simp only [VersionSpace.versionSpace, Set.mem_setOf_eq, Concept.extent_ofAttributes]
  rw [← c'.lowerPolar_intent]
  simp [Set.subset_def, Set.disjoint_left, Option.forall, mem_lowerPolar_iff, and_and_and_comm,
    -Concept.lowerPolar_intent]

/-- `mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff_ofAttributes` when the new object is not labeled. -/
private lemma mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_some_iff
    {c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (some '' N) ↔
      Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N := by
  simp [mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff_ofAttributes, Set.preimage_image_eq,
    Option.some_injective]

/-- Adding a statement that is neither positive nor negative keeps every answer:
`addObjectConcept S c` is consistent with the positive objects `Q` and the negative objects `N`
if and only if `c` is. -/
theorem addObjectConcept_mem_consistentConcepts_iff {c : Concept α β r} :
    addObjectConcept S c ∈ consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (some '' N) ↔
      c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N := by
  rw [mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_some_iff, intent_addObjectConcept,
    Concept.ofAttributes_intent]

/-- Adding a statement does not change the description of the least general generalization of
the positive objects: the concept generated by `Q` in the extended context is
`addObjectConcept S` of the concept generated by `Q`. So when consistent concepts exist, the old
certain predictions stay certain predictions, and the new object is one exactly when it has
every attribute common to the objects of `Q`. -/
theorem ofObjects_addObject :
    Concept.ofObjects (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) =
      addObjectConcept S (Concept.ofObjects r Q) :=
  Concept.ext' (Set.ext fun _ => by simp [mem_upperPolar_iff])

/-- An intent with one more object is the intent it generates in `r`, or that intent `∩ S`. -/
private lemma intent_eq_or_eq_inter_of_addObject
    (c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)) :
    c'.intent = (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent).intent ∨
      c'.intent = (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent).intent ∩ S := by
  rcases Order.isIntent_addObject_iff.1 c'.isIntent_intent with h | ⟨B, hB, h⟩
  · exact .inl h.eq.symm
  · refine .inr ((Set.subset_inter (subset_upperPolar_lowerPolar r _)
      (h ▸ Set.inter_subset_right)).antisymm ?_)
    rw [h]
    exact Set.inter_subset_inter_left S (hB.upperPolar_lowerPolar_subset Set.inter_subset_left)

/-- The answers after adding a statement that is neither positive nor negative: a concept of the
context extended by one new object with attribute set `S` is consistent with `Q` and `N` if and
only if, for some concept `c` of `r` consistent with `Q` and `N`, either it is
`addObjectConcept S c`, or its intent is `c.intent ∩ S` and no object of `N` has every attribute
of `c.intent ∩ S`. This is `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_iff` for the one-object context of `S`,
whose intents are `Set.univ` and `S` (`Order.isIntent_const_iff`), transported along
`Order.addObject_eq_sumElim`; it refines the description of the intents of the extended context
(`Order.isIntent_addObject_iff`). -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff
    {c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (some '' N) ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N, c' = addObjectConcept S c ∨
        c'.intent = c.intent ∩ S ∧ Disjoint (lowerPolar r (c.intent ∩ S)) N := by
  rw [mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_some_iff]
  refine ⟨fun hc => ⟨_, hc, ?_⟩, ?_⟩
  · rcases intent_eq_or_eq_inter_of_addObject S c' with h | h
    · exact .inl (Concept.ext' h)
    · refine .inr ⟨h, ?_⟩
      rw [← h]
      exact hc.2
  · rintro ⟨c, hc, rfl | ⟨h, hN⟩⟩
    · rwa [intent_addObjectConcept, Concept.ofAttributes_intent]
    · rw [h]
      exact ⟨subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent hc Set.inter_subset_left, hN⟩

/-- Adding a statement that is neither positive nor negative does not change whether some concept
is consistent with `Q` and `N`. -/
theorem nonempty_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff :
    (consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (some '' N)).Nonempty ↔
      (consistentConcepts r Q N).Nonempty :=
  ⟨fun ⟨_, hc'⟩ => ((mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff S).1 hc').imp fun _ h => h.1,
    fun ⟨_, hc⟩ => ⟨_, (addObjectConcept_mem_consistentConcepts_iff S).2 hc⟩⟩

/-- Each new answer is `addObjectConcept S c` or has intent `c.intent ∩ S`, for an old answer. -/
private lemma consistentConcepts_addObject_subset :
    consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (some '' N) ⊆
      addObjectConcept S '' consistentConcepts r Q N ∪
        (fun c => Concept.ofAttributes (Order.addObject r S) (c.intent ∩ S)) ''
          consistentConcepts r Q N :=
  fun _ hc' => ((mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff S).1 hc').elim fun c ⟨hc, h⟩ =>
    h.elim (fun h => .inl ⟨c, hc, h.symm⟩) fun h =>
      .inr ⟨c, hc, (congrArg _ h.1).symm.trans Concept.ofAttributes_intent⟩

/-- The map `addObjectConcept S` is injective, since it preserves intents. -/
private lemma addObjectConcept_inj {c d : Concept α β r} :
    addObjectConcept S c = addObjectConcept S d ↔ c = d :=
  ⟨fun h => Concept.ext' (congrArg Concept.intent h :), congrArg _⟩

/-- Adding a statement that is neither positive nor negative at most doubles the number of
consistent concepts, as adding an object at most doubles the number of concepts
(`Concept.card_addObject_le`). This is `card_consistentConcepts_sumElim_le` for the one-object
context of `S`, which has at most two concepts, transported along `Order.addObject_eq_sumElim`. No
finiteness is needed: if infinitely many concepts are consistent, infinitely many are consistent
after adding the statement, and both sides are `0`. -/
theorem card_consistentConcepts_addObject_le :
    Nat.card (consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (some '' N)) ≤
      2 * Nat.card (consistentConcepts r Q N) := by
  rcases (consistentConcepts r Q N).finite_or_infinite with h | h
  · rw [Nat.card_coe_set_eq, Nat.card_coe_set_eq, two_mul]
    exact (Set.ncard_le_ncard (consistentConcepts_addObject_subset S)
      ((h.image _).union (h.image _))).trans ((Set.ncard_union_le _ _).trans
        (add_le_add (Set.ncard_image_le h) (Set.ncard_image_le h)))
  · rw [h.card_eq_zero, mul_zero, (Set.infinite_of_injOn_mapsTo
      (fun _ _ _ _ => (addObjectConcept_inj S).1)
      (fun _ => (addObjectConcept_mem_consistentConcepts_iff S).2) h).card_eq_zero]

/-- The old objects of `insert none (some '' s)` are the objects of `s`. -/
private lemma preimage_some_insert_none (s : Set α) : some ⁻¹' insert none (some '' s) = s := by
  grind

/-- An intent with one more object inside `S` is the intent it generates in `r`, `∩ S`. -/
private lemma intent_eq_inter_of_addObject {c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)}
    (h : c'.intent ⊆ S) : c'.intent = (Concept.ofAttributes r c'.intent).intent ∩ S := by
  rcases intent_eq_or_eq_inter_of_addObject S c' with h' | h'
  · rw [← h']
    exact (Set.inter_eq_left.2 h).symm
  · exact h'

/-- Adding a statement as a positive object: a concept of the context extended by one new object
with attribute set `S` is consistent with the positive objects `Q` and the new object, and with
the negative objects `N`, if and only if its intent is `c.intent ∩ S` for a concept `c` of `r`
consistent with `Q` and `N` such that no object of `N` has every attribute of `c.intent ∩ S`. This
is `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_positive_iff` for the one-object context of `S`, whose object
has exactly the attributes in `S`, transported along `Order.addObject_eq_sumElim`. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_positive_iff
    {c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (insert none (some '' Q)) (some '' N) ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N,
        c'.intent = c.intent ∩ S ∧ Disjoint (lowerPolar r (c.intent ∩ S)) N := by
  rw [mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff_ofAttributes, preimage_some_insert_none,
    Set.preimage_image_eq _ (Option.some_injective α)]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨⟨hS, _⟩, hc⟩ =>
      ⟨_, hc, intent_eq_inter_of_addObject S (hS (Set.mem_insert _ _)), ?_⟩,
    fun ⟨c, hc, h, hN⟩ => ⟨⟨fun _ => h.trans_subset Set.inter_subset_right,
      fun _ ⟨_, _, h'⟩ => Option.some_ne_none _ h'⟩, ?_⟩⟩
  · rw [← intent_eq_inter_of_addObject S (hS (Set.mem_insert _ _))]
    exact hc.2
  · rw [h]
    exact ⟨subset_lowerPolar_of_subset_intent hc Set.inter_subset_left, hN⟩

/-- The least general generalization of the positive objects together with a new positive
statement with attribute set `S`: the concept they generate in the extended context has intent
`upperPolar r Q ∩ S`, the attributes common to `Q` that the new statement has. -/
theorem intent_ofObjects_addObject_insert_none :
    (Concept.ofObjects (Order.addObject r S) (insert none (some '' Q))).intent =
      upperPolar r Q ∩ S :=
  Set.ext fun _ => by simp [mem_upperPolar_iff, and_comm]

/-- After adding a statement with attribute set `S` as a positive object, some concept is
consistent if and only if no object of `N` has every attribute common to the objects of `Q`
that the new statement has. -/
theorem nonempty_consistentConcepts_addObject_positive_iff :
    (consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (insert none (some '' Q)) (some '' N)).Nonempty ↔
      Disjoint (lowerPolar r (upperPolar r Q ∩ S)) N := by
  rw [nonempty_consistentConcepts_iff, ← Concept.lowerPolar_intent,
    intent_ofObjects_addObject_insert_none]
  simp [Set.disjoint_right, mem_lowerPolar_iff]

/-- Adding a statement as a negative object: a concept of the context extended by one new object
with attribute set `S` is consistent with the positive objects `Q`, and with the negative objects
`N` and the new object, if and only if it is `addObjectConcept S c` for a concept `c` of `r`
consistent with `Q` and `N` whose intent is not contained in `S`: the old answers that the new
statement does not fit. This is `mem_consistentConcepts_sumElim_negative_iff` for the one-object
context of `S`, transported along `Order.addObject_eq_sumElim`. -/
theorem mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_negative_iff
    {c' : Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S)} :
    c' ∈ consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (insert none (some '' N)) ↔
      ∃ c ∈ consistentConcepts r Q N, ¬c.intent ⊆ S ∧ c' = addObjectConcept S c := by
  rw [mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_iff_ofAttributes, preimage_some_insert_none,
    Set.preimage_image_eq _ (Option.some_injective α)]
  refine ⟨fun ⟨⟨_, hS⟩, hc⟩ => ⟨_, hc, ?_⟩, fun ⟨c, hc, hS, h⟩ => ?_⟩
  · rcases intent_eq_or_eq_inter_of_addObject S c' with h | h
    · exact ⟨fun h' => hS (h.trans_subset h') (Set.mem_insert _ _), Concept.ext' h⟩
    · exact absurd (Set.mem_insert _ _) (hS (h.trans_subset Set.inter_subset_right))
  · subst h
    exact ⟨⟨fun ⟨_, _, h'⟩ => absurd h' (Option.some_ne_none _), fun h' => absurd h' hS⟩,
      by rwa [intent_addObjectConcept, Concept.ofAttributes_intent]⟩

/-- After adding a statement with attribute set `S` as a negative object, some concept is
consistent if and only if some concept was consistent with `Q` and `N` and the new statement
lacks some attribute common to the objects of `Q`. -/
theorem nonempty_consistentConcepts_addObject_negative_iff :
    (consistentConcepts (Order.addObject r S) (some '' Q) (insert none (some '' N))).Nonempty ↔
      Disjoint (Concept.ofObjects r Q).extent N ∧ ¬upperPolar r Q ⊆ S :=
  ⟨fun ⟨_, hc'⟩ => ((mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_negative_iff S).1 hc').elim
      fun c ⟨hc, hS, _⟩ => ⟨nonempty_consistentConcepts_iff.1 ⟨c, hc⟩,
        fun h => hS ((mem_consistentConcepts_iff_intent.1 hc).1.trans h)⟩,
    fun ⟨hd, hS⟩ => ⟨_, (mem_consistentConcepts_addObject_negative_iff S).2
      ⟨_, (isLeast_ofObjects (nonempty_consistentConcepts_iff.2 hd)).1, hS, rfl⟩⟩⟩

variable {S} in
/-- Stating an answer changes no intent: if the attribute set `S` of the new object is an intent
of `r`, the intents of the extended context are the intents of `r`, since the new intents
`B ∩ S` are intersections of intents (`Order.isIntent_addObject_iff`). -/
theorem isIntent_addObject_iff_of_isIntent (hS : Order.IsIntent r S) {T : Set β} :
    Order.IsIntent (Order.addObject r S) T ↔ Order.IsIntent r T :=
  Order.isIntent_addObject_iff.trans
    ⟨fun h => h.elim id fun ⟨_, hB, hT⟩ => hT ▸ hB.inter hS, .inl⟩

/-- The map `addObjectConcept S` preserves and reflects the order, since it preserves intents. -/
theorem addObjectConcept_le_addObjectConcept_iff {c d : Concept α β r} :
    addObjectConcept S c ≤ addObjectConcept S d ↔ c ≤ d := by
  simp [← Concept.intent_subset_intent_iff]

variable {S} in
/-- Stating an answer: if the attribute set `S` of the new object is an intent of `r`, the concept
lattice of the extended context is the concept lattice of `r`, by the intent-preserving order
isomorphism `addObjectConcept S`. -/
def addObjectOrderIso (hS : Order.IsIntent r S) :
    Concept α β r ≃o Concept (Option α) β (Order.addObject r S) where
  toFun := addObjectConcept S
  invFun c' := Concept.ofIsIntent r c'.intent
    ((isIntent_addObject_iff_of_isIntent hS).1 c'.isIntent_intent)
  left_inv _ := Concept.ext' rfl
  right_inv _ := Concept.ext' rfl
  map_rel_iff' := addObjectConcept_le_addObjectConcept_iff S

variable {S} in
/-- The isomorphism `addObjectOrderIso` is `addObjectConcept S`. -/
@[simp]
theorem addObjectOrderIso_apply (hS : Order.IsIntent r S) (c : Concept α β r) :
    addObjectOrderIso hS c = addObjectConcept S c :=
  rfl

/-- The concept of the new statement: if `c` has intent `S`, then `addObjectConcept S c` is the
object concept of the new object. -/
theorem addObjectConcept_eq_ofObject_none {c : Concept α β r} (hc : c.intent = S) :
    addObjectConcept S c = Concept.ofObject (Order.addObject r S) none :=
  Concept.ext' (by simp [hc, Set.ext_iff])

/-- The object concept of an old object is `addObjectConcept S` of its object concept in `r`. -/
private lemma ofObject_addObject_some (a : α) :
    Concept.ofObject (Order.addObject r S) (some a) = addObjectConcept S (Concept.ofObject r a) :=
  Concept.ext' (Set.ext fun _ => by simp)

/-- The object concept of the new object is `addObjectConcept S c` iff `c` has intent `S`. -/
private lemma ofObject_addObject_none_eq_iff {c : Concept α β r} :
    Concept.ofObject (Order.addObject r S) none = addObjectConcept S c ↔ c.intent = S :=
  ⟨fun h => (congrArg Concept.intent h :).symm.trans (Set.ext fun _ => mem_upperPolar_singleton _),
    fun hc => (addObjectConcept_eq_ofObject_none S hc).symm⟩

/-- Stating an unstated answer: `addObjectConcept S c` is unstated in the extended context if and
only if `c` is unstated in `r` and its intent is not the attribute set `S` of the new
statement. -/
theorem isUnstated_addObjectConcept_iff {c : Concept α β r} :
    IsUnstated (Order.addObject r S) (addObjectConcept S c) ↔
      IsUnstated r c ∧ c.intent ≠ S := by
  simp [IsUnstated, Option.exists, ofObject_addObject_some, addObjectConcept_inj,
    ofObject_addObject_none_eq_iff, and_comm]

/-- After stating `c`, the objects of its concept are the new object and those of `c`. -/
private lemma setOf_ofObject_addObject_eq {c : Concept α β r} (hc : c.intent = S) :
    {o | Concept.ofObject (Order.addObject r S) o = addObjectConcept S c} =
      insert none (some '' {a | Concept.ofObject r a = c}) :=
  Set.ext fun o => by
    cases o <;> simp [ofObject_addObject_some, addObjectConcept_inj, ofObject_addObject_none_eq_iff,
      hc]

-- `Finite α` is needed: for infinite `α` every mass in the extended context is `0`, while the
-- right side is positive.
/-- After stating the answer `c`, by adding a statement whose attribute set is the intent of `c`,
the mass of the concept of `c` is the share of the objects whose object concept it is, the new
statement included. -/
theorem mass_addObjectConcept_of_intent_eq [Finite α] {c : Concept α β r} (hc : c.intent = S) :
    Concept.mass (Order.addObject r S) (addObjectConcept S c) =
      (({a | Concept.ofObject r a = c}.ncard : ℝ) + 1) / ((Nat.card α : ℝ) + 1) := by
  rw [Concept.mass, setOf_ofObject_addObject_eq S hc, Set.ncard_insert_of_notMem (by simp),
    Set.ncard_image_of_injective _ (Option.some_injective α), Finite.card_option]
  simp

-- `Finite α` is needed, as for `mass_addObjectConcept_of_intent_eq`.
/-- After stating the answer `c`, its concept has positive mass. -/
theorem mass_addObjectConcept_pos [Finite α] {c : Concept α β r} (hc : c.intent = S) :
    0 < Concept.mass (Order.addObject r S) (addObjectConcept S c) := by
  rw [mass_addObjectConcept_of_intent_eq S hc]
  exact div_pos (Nat.cast_add_one_pos _) (Nat.cast_add_one_pos _)

end AddObject

end MathlibLattice.Query
